  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Critical Fire Weather Conditions across Northern Rockies, this\n  afternoon through tonight. \n\n- Choppy wave activity on area lakes for this afternoon and\n  evening. \n\n- Relatively cool air mass in place tomorrow through early next \n  week, but summer warmth returns thereafter.\n\nThe warm ridge of high pressure currently over the Northern\nRockies will provide one more day of hot afternoon temperatures.\nAnticipate most low elevations warming into the 90s and 100s (F).\nDespite these temperatures being in line with recent trends,\nremember to take take frequent breaks, drink plenty of water, seek\nshade, and never leaving any person or pet in a parked vehicle. \n\nThe ridge of high pressure is anticipated to break down tonight\nand tomorrow, resulting in gusty winds and low relative humidity\nacross the Northern Rockies. Red Flag conditions will develop at\nmany locations. Those recreating on area lakes, particularly\nFlathead Lake, will experience choppy wave activity this afternoon\nthrough late evening. A cold front is expected to sweep across the\nNorthern Rockies this evening. \n\nA relatively cool air mass is projected to rush over the Northern\nRockies tonight through tomorrow. Gusty winds will return during\ndaytime tomorrow and once again increase wave activity on area\nlakes. However, the cooler daytime temperatures should keep\nafternoon humidity a bit higher compared to today. \n\nPrepare for very efficient radiational cooling across the Northern\nRockies overnight tomorrow into Monday morning. High mountain\nvalleys will cool to the 30s in many locations by Monday morning. \n\nLong range ensembles generally agree that a warm high pressure\nridge will return to the Northern Rockies around the middle of\nnext week. Models also tend to agree that the next iteration of\nour summertime ridge will be several degrees cooler than the\ncurrent ridge.",
